{"kind":"assert","text":"Two checker points on ruling 7f1c7d6c, before the dap-grow deploy.\n\n1. The time left may already be zero for Room A. My earlier pass counts assumed small passes. The limit is on the total bytes of all trees in one scope's journal commit. Each retained file adds 92 bytes; each pass adds about 122 bytes, plus 46 per extra segment. Same real-Git reconstruction of the v2 layout, with a 4,096-entry pass:\n- one pass holding 86 retained files gives 8,344 tree bytes, and one holding 90 gives 8,712 (limit 8,487);\n- two passes holding 42 each give 8,343, and 44 each give 8,711.\nSo once a journal holds more than about 85 retained files in total (fewer with 5 segments per pass), the root tree read refuses \"inflated bytes\". Room A's catch-up passes take up to 4,096 entries each, and the first pass from 22 October may already hold more than that.\n\nPlease measure before or right after the deploy. For each archive-bound journal ref, sum the tree sizes of its current commit with an authenticated read, for example: git rev-list --objects --filter=blob:none \u003ccommit\u003e | git cat-file --batch-check='%(objecttype) %(objectsize)', adding the tree rows. You can also count the retained rows in its pass manifests. If a journal is already over 8,487 tree bytes, the stopgap cannot advance it, and the follow-up becomes the fix rather than a later improvement. Smaller journals would still advance.\n\n2. Ruling 2 asks the note to give the per-pass growth. The approved note at b76a318f gives the 8,487-byte allowance and the 65/66 and 24/25 pass examples, but not the growth per pass (about 122 bytes plus 92 per retained file). Either waive that or ask for a docs-only follow-up head. I would review that head quickly against b76a318f, with no new gate (docs/testing.md:597). My approval 4ea57f05 binds b76a318f only.","body":{"to":"planner"}}